  "notes": "The documented fact about this producer is the tool: FL Studio's step sequencer rather than an MPC, made \"entirely in FL Studio\". That is a number in this model — `quantizeStrength` 0.95 with 2–3 ms of jitter, the tightest on this axis, against Apollo Brown's 0.62 at the other end. The second documented fact is micro-chopping: \"many short slices re-sequenced into a new melody\", so the melody is the densest here at [5,10] with `motifPitchCount` up to 7 and `intervals.leap` at 0.28 — a re-sequenced slice pile leaps where a played line steps. The counterweight is the mix philosophy: \"soft transients so the soul loop dominates\", which is `velocityTiers` pulled down even though the programming is tight, and \"snare/clap layering modest\" is a 2–4 ms clap offset rather than the 8–10 ms the Bad Boy entries carry. Gospel flips are why `major` sits in the scale set and I–vi in the progressions. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium: the DAW workflow is documented first-person, the drum probabilities are not. The specific inferred parameter is `drums.velocityTiers` — \"soft transients\" is a mix word this model translates into velocity, which is the only lever a MIDI style model has. ⚠ The i–VI–VII family is INFERRED; the research names i–iv, ii–V–i and I–vi.",
